1
00:00:01,048 --> 00:00:05,585
Season four of <i>Downton Abbey</i>
left us with so many burning questions.

2
00:00:05,587 --> 00:00:09,488
Will Lady Mary pick Tony Gillingham
or Charles Blake?

3
00:00:09,490 --> 00:00:11,090
Let battle commence.

4
00:00:11,092 --> 00:00:15,127
Did Mr. Bates kill Mr. Green,
and if so, will he be caught?

5
00:00:15,129 --> 00:00:16,604
You know me, when I do a thing,

6
00:00:16,628 --> 00:00:18,753
I like to have a very
good reason for doing it.

7
00:00:18,777 --> 00:00:21,137
Will Edith be able
to see her daughter,

8
00:00:21,160 --> 00:00:23,469
and whatever
happened to Gregson?

9
00:00:23,471 --> 00:00:26,339
I want to know what's happened...

10
00:00:26,341 --> 00:00:31,310
If he's trapped somewhere or
falsely imprisoned...

11
00:00:31,312 --> 00:00:33,045
Or even dead.

12
00:00:33,047 --> 00:00:37,350
Will Carson and Mrs. Hughes
finally walk down the aisle?

13
00:00:37,352 --> 00:00:41,199
As a matter fact,
I know there is a wedding in season five,

14
00:00:41,223 --> 00:00:42,843
but whose wedding?

15
00:00:44,791 --> 00:00:47,326
Ooh, I couldn't ever answer that.

16
00:00:47,461 --> 00:00:50,199
- So many questions.
- I need the answers.

17
00:00:50,223 --> 00:00:52,149
Something to look forward to.

18
00:00:52,172 --> 00:00:55,167
Please join me, Bernadette Peters,
for a loving look back

19
00:00:55,169 --> 00:00:58,225
at the first four
seasons of <i>Downton Abbey</i>

20
00:00:58,248 --> 00:01:01,489
and a tantalizing and exclusive
peek at season five

21
00:01:01,506 --> 00:01:04,206
in <i>Downton Abbey Rediscovered</i>.

22
00:01:04,207 --> 00:01:08,314
Sync by f1nc0
Corrected by icephoenix
www.MY-SUBS.com

23
00:01:08,316 --> 00:01:12,865
Hi, I'm Bernadette Peters.
I'm a huge fan of <i>Downton Abbey</i>.

24
00:01:12,886 --> 00:01:17,080
I just love the way they weave together
romance, intrigue, and drama,

25
00:01:17,092 --> 00:01:21,170
so many great characters,
wonderful writing, and marvelous acting,

26
00:01:21,193 --> 00:01:24,830
and so many urgent questions
that need answers.

27
00:01:24,832 --> 00:01:27,235
Season five holds those answers,

28
00:01:27,259 --> 00:01:31,737
but will there even be
a Downton Abbey?

29
00:01:31,739 --> 00:01:34,540
Take a look at this season five clip.

30
00:01:43,682 --> 00:01:45,020
Fire!

31
00:01:45,040 --> 00:01:46,437
- What?
- Fire!

32
00:01:46,460 --> 00:01:48,187
Oh, my God, George.

33
00:02:02,569 --> 00:02:05,169
I got Sibby and George.

34
00:02:07,141 --> 00:02:09,724
Thank God.
Mary, you take them.

35
00:02:09,744 --> 00:02:10,643
Tony, go with her.

36
00:02:10,645 --> 00:02:11,911
Tom, come with me.

37
00:02:11,913 --> 00:02:14,461
You know where the sand buckets
are kept.

38
00:02:15,883 --> 00:02:18,173
Good God!
Barrow, is she all right?!

39
00:02:18,197 --> 00:02:19,535
I think so, M'Lord.

40
00:02:19,554 --> 00:02:21,554
We must get her outside!

41
00:02:21,556 --> 00:02:23,231
Quickly, Tom!

42
00:02:25,875 --> 00:02:28,587
Oh, my goodness,
was that Edith's room,

43
00:02:28,611 --> 00:02:31,497
and was Thomas
actually saving the day?

44
00:02:31,499 --> 00:02:35,201
Looks like <i>Downton Abbey</i>
is off to a dramatic start,

45
00:02:35,203 --> 00:02:37,036
and you know
we all love the drama.

46
00:02:37,038 --> 00:02:40,639
Our program is called
<i>Downton Abbey Rediscovered</i> because

47
00:02:40,641 --> 00:02:43,642
there have been so many twists
and turns after four seasons

48
00:02:43,644 --> 00:02:47,776
that looking back at the plots,
characters, and spectacular locations

49
00:02:47,800 --> 00:02:50,783
is a little like
rediscovering an old friend.

50
00:02:50,785 --> 00:02:54,854
We just can't stop talking
about its most memorable moments.

51
00:02:54,856 --> 00:02:55,922
Yes.

52
00:02:55,945 --> 00:02:57,201
I won't allow it!

53
00:02:57,225 --> 00:03:00,606
- Thank God.
- Yes. Thank God.

54
00:03:00,630 --> 00:03:02,862
In these moments,

55
00:03:02,864 --> 00:03:07,133
you can normally find
an Italian who isn't too picky.

56
00:03:07,135 --> 00:03:10,569
So much has happened both
upstairs and downstairs during

57
00:03:10,571 --> 00:03:13,739
the first four seasons of
<i>Downton Abbey</i> that I think a

58
00:03:13,741 --> 00:03:17,343
bit made a reminder of our
favorite characters at the start

59
00:03:17,345 --> 00:03:19,945
of the series might he helpful.

60
00:03:19,947 --> 00:03:24,016
Downton Abbey is the ancestral
home of the Crawley family,

61
00:03:24,018 --> 00:03:27,420
which always seems to be
on the verge of going under.

62
00:03:27,422 --> 00:03:32,162
Robert, the current Earl of Grantham,
marries an American heiress, Cora,

63
00:03:32,185 --> 00:03:35,696
to save Downton Abbey
and has three lovely daughters,

64
00:03:35,719 --> 00:03:41,100
Lady Mary, Lady Edith, and Lady Sybil,
and no direct heir.

65
00:03:41,102 --> 00:03:45,372
Downstairs,
we have tradition-bound butler Mr. Carson

66
00:03:45,373 --> 00:03:49,642
and kind head housekeeper
Mrs. Hughes running the show.

67
00:03:49,644 --> 00:03:54,613
Strict but caring cook Mrs. Patmore
and her kitchen maid Daisy.

68
00:03:54,615 --> 00:03:56,284
We should go out to greet them.

69
00:03:56,307 --> 00:03:59,360
- And me, Mr. Carson?
- No, Daisy, not you.

70
00:03:59,384 --> 00:04:02,209
Ambitious and evil
Thomas the footman,

71
00:04:02,232 --> 00:04:04,999
handsome Irish
chauffeur Branson,

72
00:04:05,023 --> 00:04:07,970
Anna, ladies' maid
to the Crawley daughters,

73
00:04:07,993 --> 00:04:12,465
and the mysterious Mr. Bates,
valet to Lord Grantham.

74
00:04:12,467 --> 00:04:16,168
Of course, there's the dowager
Countess of Grantham, Violet,

75
00:04:16,170 --> 00:04:20,039
played with so much breathtaking
aplomb by Dame Maggie Smith.

76
00:04:20,041 --> 00:04:23,754
Her quips are quite something and
only getting better with each season.

77
00:04:25,518 --> 00:04:28,614
- Good heavens. What am I sitting on?
- Uh, swivel chair.

78
00:04:28,616 --> 00:04:32,017
Is this an instrument of
communication or torture?

79
00:04:32,019 --> 00:04:34,987
What...
Hello?!

80
00:04:34,989 --> 00:04:38,057
When you talk like that,
I'm tempted to ring for Nanny

81
00:04:38,059 --> 00:04:42,928
and have you put to bed
with no supper.

82
00:04:42,930 --> 00:04:45,664
That is all I have
to say on the subject.

83
00:04:49,936 --> 00:04:52,538
One of the reasons
we love <i>Downton Abbey</i>

84
00:04:52,539 --> 00:04:55,140
is the period and
culture in which it's set.

85
00:04:55,142 --> 00:04:59,347
An aristocratic family,
the Crawleys, living large

86
00:04:59,370 --> 00:05:02,074
in an enormous English country house

87
00:05:02,097 --> 00:05:06,752
with a cadre of servants
to tend to their every need.

88
00:05:06,754 --> 00:05:11,288
When William the Conqueror
defeated the Saxons in 1066,

89
00:05:11,311 --> 00:05:15,227
he established a feudal system,
and ever since that date,

90
00:05:15,229 --> 00:05:17,947
great families gained
huge amounts of land

91
00:05:17,971 --> 00:05:21,066
and established power
and influence through it.

92
00:05:21,082 --> 00:05:24,793
And so the Crawleys,
who emerged much, much later on

93
00:05:24,816 --> 00:05:27,824
had, in their own way,
stepped into this tradition

94
00:05:27,847 --> 00:05:32,126
of having huge influence
over a large amount of land,

95
00:05:32,150 --> 00:05:35,749
and it was rather like
ruling a small country

96
00:05:35,772 --> 00:05:39,355
because not only did they have
their own economy within that,

97
00:05:39,378 --> 00:05:44,156
they were, in their own sense,
the rulers of the people who lived there.

98
00:05:44,179 --> 00:05:47,092
You do not love the place yet.

99
00:05:47,094 --> 00:05:50,262
- Well, obviously, it's...
- No, you don't love it.

100
00:05:50,264 --> 00:05:52,735
You see a million bricks
that may crumble,

101
00:05:52,758 --> 00:05:55,236
a thousand gutters and pipes
that may block and leak,

102
00:05:55,259 --> 00:05:58,437
and stone that will crack
in the frost.

103
00:05:58,439 --> 00:06:00,497
But you don't.

104
00:06:02,011 --> 00:06:04,055
I see my life's work.

105
00:06:05,446 --> 00:06:07,980
We picked the setting
of the first series originally

106
00:06:07,982 --> 00:06:10,158
at the very height
of the British empire,

107
00:06:10,182 --> 00:06:14,153
the very height
of the aristocratic system.

108
00:06:14,155 --> 00:06:17,007
It never got any better than that
for those who were at the top,

109
00:06:17,030 --> 00:06:19,336
and then of course the
First World War came and

110
00:06:19,359 --> 00:06:21,093
smashed everything,
changed everything,

111
00:06:21,116 --> 00:06:22,208
the world would
never be the same again.

112
00:06:22,232 --> 00:06:26,742
Our story begins with
the sinking of the <i>Titanic</i> in 1912

113
00:06:26,765 --> 00:06:28,968
which kills a Crawley cousin

114
00:06:28,970 --> 00:06:31,103
and leaves Matthew Crawley,

115
00:06:31,105 --> 00:06:34,573
a more distant cousin,
heir to the title.

116
00:06:34,575 --> 00:06:37,977
- It's from Lord Grantham.
- Really?

117
00:06:37,979 --> 00:06:40,337
What on earth does he want?

118
00:06:41,875 --> 00:06:43,660
He wants to change our lives.

119
00:06:43,684 --> 00:06:47,086
Matthew arrives at Downton
with his mother, Mrs. Crawley.

120
00:06:47,088 --> 00:06:49,762
The Great War changes
the entire world

121
00:06:49,786 --> 00:06:52,458
and shakes the very foundation
of Downton Abbey.

122
00:06:52,460 --> 00:06:56,395
Women are training for work,
technology advances,

123
00:06:56,397 --> 00:07:01,567
and the ancient social order
begins to crumble upstairs and down.

124
00:07:01,569 --> 00:07:06,338
Youngest Crawley daughter Lady Sybil
scandalously falls for the

125
00:07:06,340 --> 00:07:09,642
family's Irish chauffeur,
Tom Branson.

126
00:07:09,644 --> 00:07:13,746
They elope, eventually return to Downton,
and have a baby.

127
00:07:13,748 --> 00:07:20,786
And tragically, Sybil dies from
complications following childbirth.

128
00:07:20,788 --> 00:07:24,323
Is there anything we should do,
Mr. Carson?

129
00:07:24,325 --> 00:07:27,894
Carry on, Daisy,
as we all must.

130
00:07:27,913 --> 00:07:30,026
After a lot of
"will they, won't they,"

131
00:07:30,050 --> 00:07:32,898
future heir to Downton,
Matthew Crawley,

132
00:07:32,900 --> 00:07:37,536
marries eldest daughter Lady Mary,
and they have a son, baby George.

133
00:07:37,538 --> 00:07:43,142
Season three ends with Matthew's
unexpected and tragic death.

134
00:07:43,144 --> 00:07:47,880
In season four, we watched Mary
transition from death to life

135
00:07:47,882 --> 00:07:51,283
as she dealt with the loss of
her beloved husband Matthew.

136
00:07:51,285 --> 00:07:54,821
We saw Edith take life by the horns

137
00:07:54,822 --> 00:07:58,357
and have to deal with the consequences.

138
00:07:58,359 --> 00:08:04,263
And we saw new romances start to
blossom from the most unexpected places.

139
00:08:04,265 --> 00:08:08,067
But one of the most disturbing
moments in season four

140
00:08:08,069 --> 00:08:12,071
was Anna's rape
by Lord Gillingham's valet, Mr. Green.

141
00:08:12,073 --> 00:08:14,940
I still have nightmares about
that scene.

142
00:08:14,942 --> 00:08:17,159
Now let me by please.

143
00:08:17,182 --> 00:08:18,955
And Bates' response?

144
00:08:18,979 --> 00:08:23,182
Did he kill Mr. Green?
He has quite the temper.

145
00:08:23,184 --> 00:08:25,884
We'll find out in season five.

146
00:08:25,886 --> 00:08:30,010
Perhaps the most asked question
is will Lady Mary find love again

147
00:08:30,033 --> 00:08:32,024
after losing the love of her life?

148
00:08:32,026 --> 00:08:35,594
And will it be Charles Blake or
Tony Gillingham?

149
00:08:35,596 --> 00:08:39,531
Or perhaps even someone else?

150
00:08:39,533 --> 00:08:44,469
Season five starts nine months on
from where we left it in the last episode,

151
00:08:44,493 --> 00:08:48,323
and it becomes quite clear

152
00:08:48,347 --> 00:08:51,715
that one of those two
has certainly been

153
00:08:51,738 --> 00:08:55,108
seeing quite a lot of Mary
in that time.

154
00:08:55,132 --> 00:08:57,645
There was a time when you were
torn between me and Charles Blake,

155
00:08:57,657 --> 00:09:01,086
but I think you've decided
and I'm the winner.

156
00:09:01,088 --> 00:09:03,021
Well,
thank goodness that's settled.

157
00:09:03,023 --> 00:09:07,860
But of course the thing about
Mary is she wants it all,

158
00:09:07,862 --> 00:09:09,925
and she's used to having it all,

159
00:09:09,948 --> 00:09:13,095
so things are never quite
plain sailing.

160
00:09:13,118 --> 00:09:15,467
It never is simple with Mary.

161
00:09:15,469 --> 00:09:18,904
She has a hard time deciding
what her heart really desires.

162
00:09:18,906 --> 00:09:21,740
Take a look at this season five clip.

163
00:09:21,742 --> 00:09:24,861
Is Charles Blake trying
to help Lady Mary,

164
00:09:24,862 --> 00:09:27,980
or is he just setting up his
advantage for her hand?

165
00:09:27,982 --> 00:09:30,749
Nothing will make me happier
than seeing you happy,

166
00:09:30,751 --> 00:09:33,685
but please be absolutely sure
before you decide.

167
00:09:33,687 --> 00:09:36,555
- Why do you say that?
- Because you're cleverer than he is.

168
00:09:36,557 --> 00:09:38,300
That might've worked
in the last century

169
00:09:38,324 --> 00:09:40,284
when ladies had to hide their brains
behind good manners

170
00:09:40,308 --> 00:09:42,427
and good breeding but not now.

171
00:09:42,429 --> 00:09:45,697
I don't agree.
I think Tony is quite as clever as I am.

172
00:09:46,755 --> 00:09:50,172
Then one of us is right.
And one is wrong.

173
00:09:50,912 --> 00:09:53,517
The thing is with Mary
is that she...

174
00:09:53,528 --> 00:09:57,789
She makes these impulsive decisions,

175
00:09:57,812 --> 00:10:01,021
and then, you know,
she'll follow through with it,

176
00:10:01,044 --> 00:10:06,218
and then afterwards discovers
that it probably wasn't a very good idea.

177
00:10:06,220 --> 00:10:11,092
I can't wait to find out what happens
with Lady Mary and her eligible bachelors,

178
00:10:11,115 --> 00:10:13,325
but what about Lady Edith?

179
00:10:13,327 --> 00:10:17,563
Unfortunately,
her life pales in comparison to Mary's.

180
00:10:17,565 --> 00:10:21,133
Her situation leaves
much to be desired.

181
00:10:21,135 --> 00:10:24,136
That might be the understatement
of the year.

182
00:10:24,138 --> 00:10:26,613
After being jilted at the altar,

183
00:10:26,636 --> 00:10:29,854
Lady Edith falls in love
with a married editor, Michael Gregson,

184
00:10:29,877 --> 00:10:33,090
and just when things
seem to be working in her favor,

185
00:10:33,102 --> 00:10:37,015
she gets pregnant,
and her suitor goes missing.

186
00:10:37,017 --> 00:10:40,622
I was happy that Edith
chose to have the child,

187
00:10:40,645 --> 00:10:44,590
a scandal still under wraps,
but I doubt for long.

188
00:10:44,614 --> 00:10:46,124
Poor Edith.

189
00:10:46,126 --> 00:10:50,144
She struck up a deal
with Mr. Drew

190
00:10:50,168 --> 00:10:53,665
that he will take care of
this child for her.

191
00:10:53,800 --> 00:10:57,421
She's obviously going to be
visiting the farmer's house a lot,

192
00:10:57,433 --> 00:11:01,540
and obviously, she has to find a way
of doing that without raising suspicion.

193
00:11:01,542 --> 00:11:04,230
And the more
she sees her own daughter,

194
00:11:04,254 --> 00:11:09,281
of course, the more she wants to see,
and that starts to give us problems.

195
00:11:09,415 --> 00:11:12,666
We've almost forgotten she's
not one of our own, haven't we, Tim?

196
00:11:12,690 --> 00:11:13,919
She's just lovely.

197
00:11:14,053 --> 00:11:17,122
- How pretty your hair is, darling.
- We sometimes wonder where she

198
00:11:17,124 --> 00:11:19,091
got her coloring,
but I never knew her parents.

199
00:11:19,093 --> 00:11:21,460
- No?
- No.

200
00:11:21,462 --> 00:11:27,022
I think that in series five,
Edith is less inclined

201
00:11:27,046 --> 00:11:31,970
to be told no and to be made unhappy
by other people's choices.

202
00:11:32,105 --> 00:11:34,226
She takes a leaf out of Mary's book...

203
00:11:34,227 --> 00:11:36,953
Although she would kill me
for saying so...

204
00:11:36,977 --> 00:11:40,512
And she actually
starts to control her own life.

205
00:11:40,514 --> 00:11:45,217
- It's very dangerous.
- It's a very risky situation.

206
00:11:45,219 --> 00:11:48,661
If someone guesses or
if she's showing too much attention,

207
00:11:48,684 --> 00:11:51,123
then it could all...
All go wrong.

208
00:11:51,125 --> 00:11:56,428
So the potential for scandal is still
very much hanging over Lady Edith.

209
00:11:56,430 --> 00:11:59,264
Here's a look at a scene from
season five.

210
00:11:59,266 --> 00:12:03,869
It looks like her secret might
not be as hidden as she hoped.

211
00:12:06,873 --> 00:12:11,043
The real reason's a good
reason to love a child, m'lady.

212
00:12:11,177 --> 00:12:13,507
Mothers should love their children.

213
00:12:15,115 --> 00:12:19,651
- How long have you known?
- Since you asked me to take her in.

214
00:12:19,653 --> 00:12:21,086
But you're quite safe.

215
00:12:21,088 --> 00:12:25,891
It's not my place to judge you,
and I wouldn't anyway.

216
00:12:25,893 --> 00:12:27,693
But I should control my feelings.

217
00:12:27,695 --> 00:12:30,729
I could say yes,
but I don't believe it's realistic.

218
00:12:30,731 --> 00:12:32,982
I don't believe you could.

219
00:12:33,005 --> 00:12:35,764
Then what are you saying?

220
00:12:35,788 --> 00:12:40,706
We need a way for you to live the truth
without telling the truth.

221
00:12:40,708 --> 00:12:44,109
I'm not sure secret like that
can be hidden for long.

222
00:12:44,111 --> 00:12:45,711
Will the family find out?

223
00:12:45,713 --> 00:12:49,281
And what will it mean for Edith
and her reputation?

224
00:12:49,283 --> 00:12:53,118
Oh, the scandal, the intrigue,
it is intoxicating, isn't it?

225
00:12:53,120 --> 00:12:56,722
And there's a so much more to
look forward to in season five.

226
00:12:56,724 --> 00:12:58,592
I can't wait.

227
00:12:58,616 --> 00:13:00,258
You've got late-flowering love.

228
00:13:00,260 --> 00:13:03,695
You have the choices in
relationships.

229
00:13:03,697 --> 00:13:08,900
You have relationships being
tested by society at large.

230
00:13:08,902 --> 00:13:10,469
You have scandal.

231
00:13:10,471 --> 00:13:14,373
And you have a marriage that
survives rough waters.

232
00:13:14,507 --> 00:13:18,977
There is a theme in the fifth season
of things from one's past

233
00:13:19,000 --> 00:13:21,046
and nothing being
quite as you expect it to be.

234
00:13:21,048 --> 00:13:24,316
So we have this, in my opinion,
absolutely delicious storyline

235
00:13:24,318 --> 00:13:30,222
where Violet had a bit of a shady past
that has been buried for a very long time.

236
00:13:30,224 --> 00:13:33,759
I mean, one way or another,
everyone goes down the aisle

237
00:13:33,761 --> 00:13:36,028
with half the story hidden.

238
00:13:36,030 --> 00:13:38,622
We've got
Sophie's character Daisy who's,

239
00:13:38,646 --> 00:13:43,457
you know, wanting to educate herself,
which I think is great,

240
00:13:43,481 --> 00:13:46,972
and she's starting to sort of
get her voice.

241
00:13:47,106 --> 00:13:50,008
"Arithmetic part one, cost accounting"?

